Copy constructor: allocate your own array, then copy the values across.
```cpp
IntBuffer(const IntBuffer& o) : data_(new int[o.n_]), n_(o.n_) {
    std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + n_, data_);
}
```
Afterwards the two objects point at different memory, so each destructor frees its own array.

Copy-and-swap reuses the copy constructor and is self-assignment safe by construction:
```cpp
IntBuffer& operator=(const IntBuffer& o) {
    IntBuffer tmp(o);               // deep copy first
    std::swap(data_, tmp.data_);    // take its array
    std::swap(n_, tmp.n_);
    return *this;                   // tmp's destructor frees our old array
}
```
